    71	High-S/low-S acceptance, bare DER versus raw signatures, nonminimal MPI encoding, ML-DSA contexts, external-μ interoperability, ML-DSA adjacent key lengths, and public-key infinity **already have coverage**. The important omissions are those identified above. The translation’s sufficiency still requires the author’s recorded ruling, as §4 itself says.
    72	
    73	The vectors do exercise the service’s actual functions: `parse_signature`, `sig_policy`, `public_key`, `signature_verifies`, `verify_bundle`, and `parse_trust`. They are not merely cryptography tests. Direct-library characterization is clearly separated in `TestLibraryBehaviour`; input construction also uses cryptography appropriately. The permissive-backend ML-DSA tests are particularly effective: removing the service gate fails them even though OpenSSL independently rejects the malformed signatures.
